  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "The hypotenuse \\(c\\) and one arm \\(a\\) of a right triangle are consecutive integers. The square of the second arm is:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(ca\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac ca\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(c+a\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(c-a\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "none of these"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "c",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1956-p39.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "By the Pythagorean theorem, \\[ b^2=c^2-a^2=(c-a)(c+a). \\] Since \\(c\\) and \\(a\\) are consecutive and \\(c\\gt a,\\) we have \\(c-a=1.\\) Hence \\(b^2=c+a.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "If the second arm is \\(b,\\) then \\(b^2=c^2-a^2\\)",
        "Factor the difference of squares and use \\(c-a=1\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
